content.text:
Short answer: bring the wrapper back. This isn’t “for bundling only” — it’s the right pattern whenever a library mutates Zod at module load. Relying on Vitest setup files can’t guarantee ordering because Vite can evaluate your source modules while building the graph. A dedicated local entry that extends Zod and is the only place you import z from solves both runtime and types.

Do this:

1) Create a single Zod entry that applies the extension once
- src/lib/z.ts
```ts
import { z as baseZ } from "zod";
import { extendZodWithOpenApi } from "zod-openapi";

// idempotent guard (handles HMR / multiple workers)
declare global {
  // eslint-disable-next-line no-var
  var __ZOD_OPENAPI_EXTENDED__: boolean | undefined;
}
if (!globalThis.__ZOD_OPENAPI_EXTENDED__) {
  extendZodWithOpenApi(baseZ);
  globalThis.__ZOD_OPENAPI_EXTENDED__ = true;
}

export const z = baseZ;
// keep all Zod types available from this module too (optional)
export * from "zod";
```

2) Import from the wrapper everywhere you use .openapi()
- Replace all imports of zod in your code that call .openapi with:
```ts
import { z } from "@/lib/z"; // or relative path
```
- You can still import types from "zod" if you want, but it’s simpler to import from "@/lib/z" since it re-exports types.

3) Keep the local TS augmentation (works with moduleResolution: "Bundler")
- src/types/zod-openapi-augment.d.ts
```ts
import "zod";
declare module "zod" {
  interface ZodType {
    openapi<T extends import("zod").ZodTypeAny>(
      this: T,
      metadata?: import("zod-openapi").ZodOpenApiMetadata<T>
    ): T;
  }
}
export {};
```
- Ensure this file is included by tsc (tsconfig.json):
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src", "test", "src/types/zod-openapi-augment.d.ts"]
}
```
Rationale: with Bundler resolution, the subpath zod-openapi/extend doesn’t reliably expose types. Your augmentation gives tsc the method while the runtime patch happens in src/lib/z.ts. You no longer need to import "zod-openapi/extend" anywhere.

4) Vitest: no special setup needed
- Remove the import of "zod-openapi/extend" from test/setup.ts. The extension is applied when any code first imports "@/lib/z".
- Your vitest.config.ts can stay as-is.

5) Prevent regressions
- Add an ESLint guard so nobody imports bare "zod" and calls .openapi:
```json
{
  "rules": {
    "no-restricted-imports": [
      "error",
      {
        "paths": [
          { "name": "zod", "message": "Import `z` from src/lib/z (extended with zod-openapi)." },
          { "name": "zod-openapi/extend", "message": "Do not import directly. Use src/lib/z." }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Why this works:
- Runtime: every schema that uses .openapi comes from the extended z instance, so there’s no TypeError, and ordering is guaranteed regardless of Vitest’s module graph resolution.
- Types: your augmentation makes .openapi visible to tsc without depending on the library’s subpath types, which can be flaky under Bundler resolution.
- It’s environment-agnostic (tsx, Vitest, prod). Calling extendZodWithOpenApi once per process is the supported usage; the global guard avoids issues with HMR/workers.

If you absolutely can’t touch imports everywhere, the only other workable approach is a Vite alias that rewrites "zod" to your wrapper just for tests — but that requires careful escape hatching inside the wrapper to import the real zod (to avoid infinite recursion) and is brittle across versions. The local wrapper import is the clean, reliable solution.
